DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain covers the philosophical traditions Hegel appropriates
and critically transforms in developing his conception of freedom:
Kantian autonomy, Rousseau's civil and natural freedom, Stoic and
Christian inner freedom, and early modern natural law (Grotius,
Pufendorf, Wolff). Scholarship divides into three broad clusters:
(1) studies of how Hegel retains Kant's core insight into freedom
as self-determination while rejecting its formalism and subject-
object dualism (Pippin 1989, Beiser 2005, Clarke 2011); (2) studies
of the Rousseau-Hegel relation, particularly how Hegel appropriates
the general will as the basis of political authority while
transforming it into an institutional-ethical framework (Neuhouser
2000, Franco 1999, Church 2010); and (3) studies of the 1802/03
Natural Law essay as the hinge between early Frankfurt writings and
mature political philosophy (Molloy 2024, Clarke 2011). Taylor
(1975) and Hardimon (1994) remain foundational for contextualizing
these appropriations within Hegel's overall project.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
Understanding which elements of Kant (self-legislation), Rousseau
(general will, civic freedom), and natural law theory Hegel retains
versus transforms is essential for grasping the internal structure
of the tripartite freedom account in the Philosophy of Right. The
debate about whether Hegel's departures from Kant are improvements
or regressive remains central to contemporary assessments of his
political philosophy.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
The specifically Stoic and Christian dimensions of freedom as they
appear in the Phenomenology (Stoic consciousness, unhappy
consciousness) receive sustained treatment mainly as passages within
broader Hegel studies; dedicated articles with full bibliographic
data are sparse in English. The Grotius-Pufendorf-Wolff natural law
background receives limited coverage in the English-language
literature compared to the Kant-Rousseau axis.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
These source-relation studies provide the diagnostic framework for
evaluating Hegel's innovations; link them to Domain 1 (positive
account) to show how Hegel's departures from Kant and Rousseau are
motivated by his conception of Sittlichkeit.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Neo-Kantian reading: Hegel extends rather than abandons Kant (5 papers)
- Rousseau-Hegel transmission: general will becomes Sittlichkeit (4 papers)
- Natural law critique as hinge: 1802/03 essay as transition (2 papers)
====================================================================
}

@book{pippin1989hegelidealism,
  author    = {Pippin, Robert B.},
  title     = {Hegel's Idealism: The Satisfactions of Self-Consciousness},
  year      = {1989},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi       = {10.1017/cbo9780511621109},
  note      = {CORE ARGUMENT: Pippin argues that Hegel's idealism is best understood as a radicalization of Kant's project of showing how self-consciousness constitutes the conditions of possible experience; freedom as self-determination is not abandoned but de-individualized and historicized. RELEVANCE: The foundational text for the neo-Kantian reading that dominates Anglophone Hegel scholarship; it establishes the interpretive framework within which all debates about Hegel's relationship to Kant's practical philosophy must be situated. POSITION: Neo-Kantian/non-metaphysical reading of Hegel's appropriation of Kantian autonomy.},
  keywords  = {hegel-kant, self-consciousness, idealism, neo-kantian, High}
}

@book{taylor1975hegel,
  author    = {Taylor, Charles},
  title     = {Hegel},
  year      = {1975},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  note      = {CORE ARGUMENT: Taylor's study situates Hegel's entire system within the post-Kantian project of overcoming subject-object dualism, tracing how Hegel transforms Kant's practical philosophy, Rousseau's social thought, and Romantic Naturphilosophie into a philosophy of Spirit as self-realizing freedom in history. RELEVANCE: The most sustained account in English of the intellectual background to Hegel's freedom concept, including sustained chapters on the Kant and Rousseau sources; essential orientation for understanding Hegel's transformative appropriations. POSITION: Expressivist reading that emphasizes continuity with Romantic sources.},
  keywords  = {hegel-kant, hegel-rousseau, intellectual-background, expressivist, High}
}

@book{beiser2005hegel,
  author    = {Beiser, Frederick},
  title     = {Hegel},
  year      = {2005},
  publisher = {Routledge},
  doi       = {10.4324/9780203087053},
  note      = {CORE ARGUMENT: Beiser provides a systematic overview of Hegel's philosophy, arguing that understanding his departures from Kant and Fichte is necessary for grasping the organic and social conception of freedom that replaces Kantian autonomy. RELEVANCE: Offers an authoritative survey of the intellectual sources --- Kant, Fichte, Schelling, Rousseau --- and situates each major Hegelian innovation against its background, making it a key reference for the domain-mapping task. POSITION: Historically grounded reading attentive to the German Idealist context.},
  keywords  = {hegel-kant, intellectual-background, survey, High}
}

@book{neuhouser2000foundations,
  author    = {Neuhouser, Frederick},
  title     = {Foundations of Hegel's Social Theory: Actualizing Freedom},
  year      = {2000},
  publisher = {Harvard University Press},
  doi       = {10.4159/9780674041455},
  note      = {CORE ARGUMENT: Neuhouser reconstructs Hegel's social theory as a theory of freedom that appropriates Rousseau's insight that freedom requires a certain kind of social relation while extending it into a three-part structure of subjective, objective, and absolute freedom instantiated in family, civil society, and state. RELEVANCE: Directly addresses how Rousseau's distinction between natural and civil freedom shapes Hegel's tripartite structure, and how Kant's practical philosophy is retained and transformed; central to the key questions of this domain. POSITION: Social-freedom reading that foregrounds the Rousseau-Hegel transmission.},
  keywords  = {hegel-rousseau, hegel-kant, social-freedom, High}
}

@book{hardimon1994hegelsocial,
  author    = {Hardimon, Michael O.},
  title     = {Hegel's Social Philosophy: The Project of Reconciliation},
  year      = {1994},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  doi       = {10.1017/cbo9780511624773},
  note      = {CORE ARGUMENT: Hardimon argues that Hegel's social philosophy is organized around the project of reconciliation --- showing that modern social institutions are rational and that citizens can be at home in them --- tracing this project back to Hegel's early engagement with Kant, Rousseau, and classical republican thought. RELEVANCE: Chapter 1 provides the most detailed account in English of Hegel's intellectual sources, making it indispensable for identifying which elements of Kant and Rousseau Hegel appropriates. POSITION: Reconciliation reading, non-metaphysical orientation.},
  keywords  = {reconciliation, hegel-kant, hegel-rousseau, intellectual-background, High}
}

@book{franco1999hegelphilosophy,
  author    = {Franco, Paul},
  title     = {Hegel's Philosophy of Freedom},
  year      = {1999},
  publisher = {Yale University Press},
  note      = {CORE ARGUMENT: Franco traces Hegel's philosophy of freedom from his early theological and political writings through the mature system, arguing that Rousseau's identification of freedom with self-governance is the single most important source for Hegel's political philosophy even as Hegel transforms it into an institutional account. RELEVANCE: Provides a focused treatment of the Rousseau-Hegel connection on the question of freedom, directly relevant to the key question of how Rousseau's natural/civil freedom distinction shapes Hegel's tripartite structure. POSITION: Liberal-republican reading emphasizing continuity with Rousseau.},
  keywords  = {hegel-rousseau, freedom, political-philosophy, High}
}

@book{ritter1982hegelfrenchrevolution,
  author    = {Ritter, Joachim},
  title     = {Hegel and the French Revolution: Essays on the Philosophy of Right},
  year      = {1982},
  publisher = {MIT Press},
  note      = {CORE ARGUMENT: Ritter argues that Hegel's political philosophy is the attempt to reconcile the French Revolution's abstract principles of freedom with the concrete ethical life of historical institutions, interpreting Hegel's critique of Rousseau and natural rights theory as a conservative-Burkean appropriation of modernity. RELEVANCE: Establishes the interpretive framework of Hegel as theorist of bourgeois modernity who both endorses and critically limits revolutionary freedom; sets the terms for later debates about Hegel's relation to natural law and social contract traditions. POSITION: Conservative-reconciliation reading; influential but contested.},
  keywords  = {hegel-rousseau, natural-law, french-revolution, political-philosophy, High}
}

@book{knowles2003hegelguide,
